WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:05.130
The source is the University of
California Irvine Bike Sharing dataset.

2
00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.490
Its creator and license are
recorded with the source files.

3
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.910
We retain its official archive and
extract the hourly file and its readme.

4
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.130
Daily records remain separate.

5
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:20.630
The acquired hourly file contains
seventeen thousand three hundred seventy

6
00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:24.170
nine observations from twenty
eleven and twenty twelve.

7
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.220
We retain the acquisition details,
attribution and license,

8
00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:30.990
together with hashes
of the original bytes.

9
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.900
This gives later comparisons a specific
dataset identity rather than only a

10
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:36.750
download link.

11
00:00:38.609 --> 00:00:43.069
In the parser,
the identifier becomes record I D.

12
00:00:43.069 --> 00:00:45.519
The date and hour become
the target timestamp.

13
00:00:46.289 --> 00:00:50.299
Validated source calendar fields
stay together in a dictionary.

14
00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:54.619
This intermediate dictionary
contains seven calendar fields.

15
00:00:55.549 --> 00:00:58.259
The observed total becomes
the separate target.

16
00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:03.619
The modelling encoder later selects five
permitted fields from that dictionary.

17
00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:09.669
This split lets the parser validate source
information without silently making every

18
00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:11.999
validated field a model input.

19
00:01:14.109 --> 00:01:15.879
Before returning a row,

20
00:01:16.129 --> 00:01:20.959
the parser checks all seventeen required
fields and their allowed values.

21
00:01:21.509 --> 00:01:23.639
Numeric values must be finite.

22
00:01:24.249 --> 00:01:27.819
The date must agree with year,
month and weekday codes.

23
00:01:28.349 --> 00:01:31.639
Working day must agree with
the weekend and holiday flags.

24
00:01:32.789 --> 00:01:34.799
Component counts must add to the total.

25
00:01:35.609 --> 00:01:37.939
The file audit checks unique identifiers,

26
00:01:38.569 --> 00:01:42.219
unique hourly labels and
strict chronological order.

27
00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:45.879
These checks establish a
coherent set of observations.

28
00:01:46.349 --> 00:01:49.859
They do not measure how well
a demand model generalizes.

29
00:01:51.915 --> 00:01:57.125
One real source jump goes from January
second at four in the morning to six.

30
00:01:57.615 --> 00:02:00.365
The five o'clock label
has no row between them.

31
00:02:00.915 --> 00:02:05.645
Two hours between recorded
labels imply one unrecorded slot,

32
00:02:05.775 --> 00:02:07.965
calculated as two minus one.

33
00:02:08.475 --> 00:02:10.125
We leave that slot absent.

34
00:02:10.855 --> 00:02:15.765
Writing a new row with target zero
would claim that no rentals occurred,

35
00:02:15.955 --> 00:02:18.025
which the file does not establish.

36
00:02:18.435 --> 00:02:24.025
Complete cells in the rows we have do
not imply a complete hourly time grid.

37
00:02:26.002 --> 00:02:30.092
The source labels span seven
hundred thirty one calendar days.

38
00:02:30.582 --> 00:02:33.152
A naive grid has twenty
four slots per day.

39
00:02:33.582 --> 00:02:36.812
That gives seventeen thousand
five hundred forty four slots.

40
00:02:37.482 --> 00:02:41.822
Subtracting seventeen thousand three
hundred seventy nine observations leaves

41
00:02:41.822 --> 00:02:46.292
one hundred sixty five unrecorded slots,
across seventy five gaps.

42
00:02:46.802 --> 00:02:51.532
Later we deliberately exclude two existing
boundary rows for forecast timing.

43
00:02:52.062 --> 00:02:53.592
Those are a different category.

44
00:02:54.222 --> 00:02:56.212
Missing rows are absent observations.

45
00:02:56.802 --> 00:03:01.122
Boundary exclusions are recorded
observations whose evaluation issue times

46
00:03:01.122 --> 00:03:02.212
are too early.

47
00:03:04.108 --> 00:03:07.058
The audit counts have
a useful denominator.

48
00:03:07.328 --> 00:03:12.348
Seven hundred thirty one days times twenty
four labels gives seventeen thousand five

49
00:03:12.348 --> 00:03:15.258
hundred forty four naive hourly slots.

50
00:03:15.888 --> 00:03:18.698
The difference from the recorded
row count is one hundred sixty five,

51
00:03:19.708 --> 00:03:21.298
about zero point nine four percent.

52
00:03:21.788 --> 00:03:25.798
That is a missing label fraction
under this grid convention.

53
00:03:26.148 --> 00:03:28.538
It does not identify the missing outcomes,

54
00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:32.538
their cause,
a time zone or a daylight saving policy.

55
00:03:32.928 --> 00:03:36.398
Those are additional claims that
these counts do not establish.

56
00:03:38.566 --> 00:03:41.416
Consider an explicitly
illustrative window:

57
00:03:41.866 --> 00:03:44.596
ten, a missing row, and thirty.

58
00:03:45.346 --> 00:03:47.596
The two observed values average twenty.

59
00:03:48.606 --> 00:03:52.981
Inserting a zero makes three values whose
average is about thirteen point three

60
00:03:52.981 --> 00:03:53.576
three.

61
00:03:54.046 --> 00:03:58.356
The code shows that both the
denominator and the dataset changed.

62
00:03:58.586 --> 00:04:00.656
We did not merely tidy a display.

63
00:04:01.366 --> 00:04:06.206
A later constant predictor or lag
calculation can change because of that

64
00:04:06.206 --> 00:04:07.276
invented outcome.

65
00:04:07.786 --> 00:04:11.516
Keep this separate from the actual
recorded demand in the source.

66
00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:18.040
Suppose every cell in every
available row passes validation.

67
00:04:18.770 --> 00:04:21.700
Does that prove that
every hourly slot exists?

68
00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:24.580
Pause and separate the two questions.

69
00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:29.300
A valid row can be followed by
another valid row two hours later.

70
00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:34.120
Cell level consistency and time
grid coverage are different checks.

71
00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:39.980
Preserve the distinction between a
recorded zero and no recorded observation.

72
00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:44.110
Choosing a justified missing
data policy is later work,

73
00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.780
rather than a silent parser assumption.
